  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Valley fog this morning gives way to abundant\nsunshine\n\nHigh pressure centered over the Ohio Valley is delivering calm\nwinds and clear skies in the presence of sufficiently moist\ndewpoints to produce widespread valley fog this morning.\nNighttime microphysics imagery depicts a classic radiational\ncooling/dendritic fog pattern in the valleys of northern PA\nwhere air-water temperature differences are plenty wide to\nsupport dense fog. We expect fog to continue to expand/spread\nsouthward through daybreak with impacts possible along/west of\nI-99 and along/north of I-80. Once the sun comes up, fog should\nmix out relatively quickly giving way to a gorgeous day.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Comfortable and dry end to an active month of\nJuly \n\nHigh pressure remains in control today and tonight under mostly\nsunny skies and a seasonably warm day. With a retreating upper\ntrough overhead, cool temps aloft will support some fair weather\ncumulus development this afternoon. Highs will range from the \nupper 70s at the highest elevations in northern and western PA \nto the upper 80s in the valleys of southeast PA. High clouds \nwill begin to increase late tonight as a broad area of low \npressure approaches from the Mississippi Valley, but no rain is \nexpected until later Saturday.\n\nJuly goes out like a lamb after a record-setting month. Our\noffice issued 12 Tornado Warnings in July, which is the most \nsince 1996 when we issued 25. 95 Severe Thunderstorm Warnings is\nthe 3rd most in any July on record, behind 1996 (96) and 2021 \n(122). Year-to-date, 226 Severe Thunderstorm Warnings is the \nmost on record, passing 2019 (221), and 24 Tornado Warnings is \n4th most behind 1998 (55), 2011 (37), and 1996 (30). 250 total \nSevere Thunderstorm Warnings + Tornado Warnings is the most on \nrecord year-to-date, passing 2019 (241).\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Rain most likely Sunday with increased risk of flooding \ndownpours and isolated strong wind gusts\n\nAn axis of anomalous PW ~1.5\" along and ahead of an approaching\ncold front combined with favorable upper dynamics should support\nactive convection reaching CPA as early as late Saturday night\nwith max POPs centered on the 24hr Sunday window. With some\npotential for training in the moist low level SW flow parallel\nto the front and increased vulnerability given recent very heavy\nrainfall, WPC introduced a level 2/4 excessive rain outlook\n(ERO) that covers northwest PA (mainly northwest of I-99/I-80).\nExact timing and placement of max QPF remains a bit unclear as \nthere are still some model differences regarding the evolution \nof the upper trough, but model guidance is starting to converge \non swaths of up to 2-4\" of rain across western PA.\n\nAlthough flooding will likely be the primary threat, sufficient\ndestabilization could result in an isolated risk of damaging\nstraight-line winds as well. Curved wind profiles could also\nsupport an isolated tornado threat in the presence of relatively\nstrong shear. The degree of clearing/destabilization will be the\nprimary predictor of any potential severe weather threat.\nMorning clouds/showers lend low confidence in much of a threat\nand a Marginal Risk (level 1 out of 5) reflects that\nuncertainty/low ceiling.",
